@node Overview
@chapter Overview

AI coding agents such as @uref{https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code, Claude Code} and @uref{https://openai.com/index/introducing-codex/, Codex} store complete conversation transcripts as JSONL files.  While these files contain a full record of every session@comma{} they are not human-readable: each line is a JSON object with nested content arrays@comma{} tool-use blocks@comma{} thinking sections@comma{} and system metadata.  @samp{agent-log.el} renders these transcripts into readable Markdown files and provides a dedicated Emacs interface for browsing@comma{} searching@comma{} and navigating them.

What counts as a session is not Agent Log's decision.  Each backend reports the conversations its own tool offers when you run its resume command@comma{} and Agent Log lists those@comma{} with the one exception for Claude Code described under @ref{Claude Code, , Claude Code data sources}.  Codex is asked directly@comma{} over app-server @samp{thread/list}; Claude Code has no such interface@comma{} so Agent Log lists its project transcript directories and applies the filters of @samp{claude --resume} (see @ref{Claude Code, , Claude Code data sources}).  This matters because both tools write transcript files that are not conversations: Codex writes a separate rollout file for every subagent@comma{} permission guardian@comma{} and non-interactive execution thread@comma{} each inheriting the parent's working directory@comma{} and Claude Code writes subagent transcripts under a @samp{subagents/} subdirectory and one transcript for every Agent SDK run.  Because neither tool lists those in its own resume picker@comma{} they never reach Agent Log.

The package supports multiple backends through a generic interface based on @code{cl-defstruct} and @code{cl-defmethod}.  Each backend knows how to discover sessions@comma{} parse its tool's JSONL format@comma{} and normalize entries into a canonical representation that the rendering pipeline@comma{} session browser@comma{} and AI features operate on.  Currently supported backends are Claude Code (@samp{agent-log-claude.el}) and Codex (@samp{agent-log-codex.el}).  By default@comma{} the package auto-detects which tools are installed and activates the corresponding backends (@ref{Backend configuration, , Backend configuration}).

The package maintains a mirror directory of pre-rendered Markdown files@comma{} organized by project.  This means that standard tools such as @samp{consult-ripgrep}@comma{} Dired@comma{} and @samp{grep} work natively on readable content without any special integration.

The core capabilities of @samp{agent-log.el} are:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Session browsing}.  A @code{completing-read} interface lists ordinary user sessions@comma{} grouped by project or as a flat list@comma{} sorted by recency.  Each candidate shows a backend-specific icon (SVG in GUI frames@comma{} a short text label in terminal frames)@comma{} the date@comma{} project name@comma{} JSONL file size@comma{} and either an AI-generated summary or the first user message (@ref{Session browsing, , Session browsing}).
@item
@strong{Lazy rendering}.  Sessions are rendered to @samp{.md} files on first access.  The package tracks JSONL file sizes in an index to avoid re-rendering files that have not changed (@ref{Sync and rendering, , Sync and rendering}).
@item
@strong{Live updates}.  For active sessions@comma{} a @code{file-notify} watcher detects JSONL changes and appends new entries to both the @samp{.md} file on disk and the open buffer in real time (@ref{Live updates, , Live updates}).
@item
@strong{Outline navigation}.  Rendered buffers use @code{outline-minor-mode} for folding tool calls@comma{} thinking blocks@comma{} and tool results.  Dedicated keybindings jump between conversation turns (@ref{Navigation, , Navigation}).
@item
@strong{Smart tool summaries}.  Each tool call is summarized by its most relevant input—the file path for Read/Write@comma{} the command for Bash@comma{} the pattern for Grep@comma{} and so on.
@item
@strong{AI-powered session summaries}.  Concise one-line and paragraph summaries for each session@comma{} generated through @samp{gptel} or through the Claude Code or Codex command-line tool you are already logged in to@comma{} displayed in the browser and at the top of rendered buffers (@ref{Summary generation, , Summary generation}).
@item
@strong{AI-powered search}.  A two-stage LLM pipeline lets you search your session history with natural language queries.  A scope-narrowing model filters sessions by project and date range@comma{} then a selection model identifies the most relevant sessions and presents a narrative with clickable links (@ref{AI search, , AI search}).
@item
@strong{Session resumption}.  You can resume any session directly in the coding agent from within the rendered buffer@comma{} provided the corresponding Emacs integration package is installed (e.g. @samp{claude-code} for Claude Code) (@ref{Session resumption, , Session resumption}).
@end itemize

The package requires Emacs 29.1 or later@comma{} @uref{https://jblevins.org/projects/markdown-mode/, markdown-mode} 2.6 or later@comma{} and @uref{https://github.com/magit/transient, transient} 0.13.3 or later.  The optional summary feature requires either @uref{https://github.com/karthink/gptel, gptel} or a logged-in Claude Code or Codex command-line tool (@ref{Summary generation (1), , Summary generation options}).  Session resumption for Claude Code sessions requires @uref{https://github.com/stevemolitor/claude-code.el, claude-code}; for Codex sessions it requires @uref{https://github.com/benthamite/codex.el, codex}.

The development repository is on @uref{https://github.com/benthamite/agent-log, GitHub}.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@menu
* Manual installation::
* Installation with use-package::
@end menu

@node Manual installation
@section Manual installation

Clone the repository to a directory on your Emacs load path and add the following to your init file:

@lisp
(require 'agent-log)
@end lisp

@node Installation with use-package
@section Installation with use-package

If you use @code{use-package}@comma{} add one of the following snippets to your init file:

@lisp
;; with vc
(use-package agent-log
  :vc (:url "https://github.com/benthamite/agent-log"))

;; with elpaca
(use-package agent-log
  :ensure (:host github :repo "benthamite/agent-log"))

;; with straight
(use-package agent-log
  :straight (:host github :repo "benthamite/agent-log"))

;; with quelpa
(use-package agent-log
  :quelpa (agent-log :fetcher github :repo "benthamite/agent-log"))
@end lisp

