@node Backend configuration
@section Backend configuration

@vindex agent-log-backends
@vindex agent-log-active-backends

The user option @code{agent-log-backends} is an alist mapping backend key symbols to their feature (file) names.  Each entry is a cons cell @samp{(KEY . FEATURE)} where @samp{KEY} is a symbol identifying the backend (e.g. @code{claude-code}@comma{} @code{codex}) and @samp{FEATURE} is the symbol passed to @code{require} to load the backend file.  Backend files are loaded lazily on first use.  The default value includes entries for Claude Code and Codex:

@lisp
'((claude-code . agent-log-claude)
  (codex . agent-log-codex))
@end lisp

You would modify this to add a custom backend or to remove a backend you do not want available.

The user option @code{agent-log-active-backends} controls which backends are scanned for sessions.  It accepts either the symbol @code{auto} or a list of backend key symbols.  When set to @code{auto} (the default)@comma{} the package checks whether each backend's root directory exists on disk (e.g. @samp{~/.claude} for Claude Code@comma{} @samp{~/.codex} for Codex) and activates only the backends whose directories are found.  Set it to an explicit list such as @code{(claude-code)} if you want to restrict the session browser to a specific backend regardless of what is installed.

@node Directory configuration
@section Directory configuration

@vindex agent-log-directory
@vindex agent-log-rendered-directory

The user option @code{agent-log-directory} specifies the root directory of the Claude Code backend's configuration.  The package lists session files under the @samp{projects/} subdirectory and reads display text from @samp{history.jsonl} in this directory.  The default value is @samp{~/.claude}.  You would change this only if you have configured Claude Code to use a non-standard location.  Each backend has its own directory configuration.

The user option @code{agent-log-rendered-directory} specifies the directory where rendered Markdown files are stored.  Each project gets its own subdirectory@comma{} and each session is rendered as a dated @samp{.md} file within it (@ref{Sync and rendering, , Sync and rendering}).  The default value is @samp{~/.claude/rendered}.  You might change this if you want rendered files to live outside the default configuration directory@comma{} for example on a path indexed by a desktop search tool.

For Codex@comma{} Agent Log follows the same account-aware home selected by the installed Agent integration.  If that integration is unavailable@comma{} it uses @samp{CODEX_HOME} and then the Codex backend's configured directory.  Session discovery@comma{} transcript lookup@comma{} and resume all use this same resolved home.

@node Display of thinking and tool sections
@section Display of thinking and tool sections

@vindex agent-log-show-thinking
@vindex agent-log-show-tools

Agent conversations contain thinking blocks (the model's chain-of-thought reasoning) and tool-use sections (file reads@comma{} bash commands@comma{} grep searches@comma{} and so on).  These are valuable for understanding what the assistant did@comma{} but they can also be verbose and clutter the view when you are reading the high-level conversation.

Note that some backends store thinking blocks with empty content.  Claude Code@comma{} for instance@comma{} redacts the thinking text from its JSONL logs@comma{} keeping only a cryptographic signature.  When the thinking text is empty@comma{} the package omits the section entirely rather than rendering an empty heading.

The user option @code{agent-log-show-thinking} controls how thinking blocks appear.  It accepts one of three symbols:

@itemize
@item
@code{hidden}: thinking blocks are omitted entirely from the rendered output.
@item
@code{collapsed}: thinking blocks are rendered but folded under a heading@comma{} so you see the heading but not the content until you expand it.
@item
@code{visible}: thinking blocks are rendered fully expanded.
@end itemize

The default value is @code{collapsed}.

The user option @code{agent-log-show-tools} works identically but controls tool-use and tool-result sections.  It also defaults to @code{collapsed}.

When both options are set to @code{hidden}@comma{} assistant turns that contain only tool calls and thinking (no visible text) are hidden entirely@comma{} including their heading and separator.

You can toggle individual sections interactively using @code{agent-log-toggle-section} (@ref{Section visibility, , Section visibility}).

@node Timestamps
@section Timestamps

@vindex agent-log-timestamp-format

The user option @code{agent-log-timestamp-format} controls the format string used when displaying timestamps in rendered output.  It follows the conventions of @code{format-time-string}.  The default value is @samp{"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"}.

@node Tool output limits
@section Tool output limits

@vindex agent-log-max-tool-input-length
@vindex agent-log-max-tool-result-length

Tool calls can have very long inputs (entire file contents for Write@comma{} lengthy commands for Bash) and even longer results.  Two user options control how much of this content is shown in the rendered output.

The user option @code{agent-log-max-tool-input-length} sets the maximum number of characters displayed for tool input summaries.  Text beyond this limit is truncated with an ellipsis.  The default value is @code{200}.

The user option @code{agent-log-max-tool-result-length} sets the maximum number of characters displayed for tool result content.  The default value is @code{500}.

Increase these values if you find that truncated tool content is hiding information you need.  Decrease them if rendered files are too large or buffers are slow to navigate.

@node Session browser options
@section Session browser options

@vindex agent-log-group-by-project
@vindex agent-log-session-sort-key
@vindex agent-log-ignored-project-regexps
@vindex agent-log-project-column-max-width
@vindex agent-log-slug-max-length

The user option @code{agent-log-group-by-project} controls whether @code{agent-log-browse-sessions} groups sessions by project (@ref{Session browsing, , Session browsing}).  When non-@code{nil}@comma{} the command first prompts for a project@comma{} then for a session within that project.  When @code{nil} (the default)@comma{} all sessions are presented in a single flat list.  The grouped view is more convenient when you have many sessions across several projects; the flat view is useful for quickly finding a recent session regardless of project.

The user option @code{agent-log-session-sort-key} controls which timestamp defines “most recent” in the session browser.  The default@comma{} @code{creation-time}@comma{} sorts by the backend's recorded session timestamp: Codex's catalog creation time or the first prompt in a Claude Code transcript.  Set it to @code{modification-time} to sort by the backend's recorded last activity: Codex's catalog update time or the latest timestamp in a Claude Code transcript.  Copying or rewriting transcript files therefore does not make old conversations appear recent.  Backends without an activity timestamp retain the file-modification-time behavior; if the source file is unavailable too@comma{} they use the recorded session timestamp.  The date column displays the timestamp used for sorting.  If a Codex transcript changes after the catalog snapshot@comma{} browsing starts a background catalog refresh; its updated activity dates appear on the next browse after that refresh completes.

The user option @code{agent-log-ignored-project-regexps} is a list of regular expressions; any session whose working directory matches one of them is omitted from the browser@comma{} @code{agent-log-open-latest}@comma{} and AI search.  The default excludes sessions run under the system temporary directory (@samp{/tmp} and @samp{/private/tmp})@comma{} which is where throwaway scratch sessions accumulate — for example when repeatedly launching a coding agent in disposable directories to test it.  Ignored sessions are not deleted: they remain on disk and can still be opened directly with @code{agent-log-open-file} or @code{agent-log-open-session}.  Add your own patterns to hide other transient or uninteresting locations.

The user option @code{agent-log-project-column-max-width} bounds the width of the project column in the session browser.  The column is normally as wide as the longest project name on display@comma{} which means a single unusually long project directory pads every other row and leaves less space for the summary.  Names wider than this option are abbreviated with an ellipsis instead.  The default value is @code{24}; set it to @code{nil} to always show every project name in full.

The user option @code{agent-log-slug-max-length} sets the maximum length of the slug portion of rendered filenames.  The slug is derived from the first user message and appears after the UTC date in the filename.  The backend and full session ID follow the slug so repeated opening text cannot collapse distinct sessions (e.g.@comma{} @samp{2026-02-22_11-56_redesign-agent-log-package--codex--019c...md}).  The default value is @code{50}.

@node Live updates
@section Live updates

@vindex agent-log-live-update

The user option @code{agent-log-live-update} controls whether the package watches the source JSONL file for changes when a rendered buffer is open.  When non-@code{nil} (the default)@comma{} a @code{file-notify} watcher detects new entries appended to the JSONL file and renders them into both the open buffer and the @samp{.md} file on disk.  This means you can have a rendered log open alongside an active coding agent session and see new turns appear as they happen.

Set this to @code{nil} if you experience performance issues with file watching@comma{} or if you are on a system where @code{file-notify} is not reliable (e.g.@comma{} certain remote filesystems via TRAMP).

@node Summary generation (1)
@section Summary generation

@vindex agent-log-summary-method
@vindex agent-log-summary-backend
@vindex agent-log-summary-model
@vindex agent-log-summary-max-content-length
@vindex agent-log-summary-claude-program
@vindex agent-log-summary-codex-program
@vindex agent-log-summary-codex-model
@vindex agent-log-summary-cli-timeout

The summary feature generates concise descriptions of each session@comma{} either through @samp{gptel} or through an agent command-line tool.

The user option @code{agent-log-summary-method} chooses how.  Its value is one of these symbols:

@table @asis
@item @code{gptel} (the default)
Send each request through @samp{gptel}@comma{} as configured by @code{agent-log-summary-backend} and @code{agent-log-summary-model} below.  This needs @samp{gptel} and an API key for its backend.
@item @code{agent-cli}
Run a command-line tool you are already logged in to@comma{} so that no API key or @samp{gptel} is needed.  Claude Code is used when it is installed and logged in (@samp{claude auth status} succeeds); it runs @samp{claude --print} with the Haiku model.  Otherwise Codex is used when it is installed and logged in (@samp{codex login status} succeeds); it runs @samp{codex exec} with low reasoning effort.  When neither tool is usable@comma{} no summary is generated@comma{} automatic summaries are suspended@comma{} and the reason (which tool is missing and which is logged out) is reported once in the echo area.  Run @code{agent-log-summarize-sessions} after fixing the cause to resume them.
@item @code{claude}
Use Claude Code only.
@item @code{codex}
Use Codex only.
@end table

The command-line tools receive the same instructions and conversation text as a @samp{gptel} request@comma{} and their reply is parsed and stored the same way@comma{} so the summaries look the same whichever method produced them.  Each run is an asynchronous subprocess@comma{} so the running Emacs never waits for it; the background summary workers@comma{} which are separate Emacs processes@comma{} wait there instead.  The runs start from @samp{temporary-file-directory}@comma{} receive no secrets@comma{} and save no transcript: Claude Code runs with @samp{--no-session-persistence} and Codex with @samp{--ephemeral}@comma{} so a summary run never shows up as a session to browse or summarize.  Should a Claude Code print-mode transcript exist anyway@comma{} it records the @samp{sdk-cli} entrypoint and is left out like any other Agent SDK run (@ref{Claude Code, , Claude Code data sources}).  To keep the runs free of side effects@comma{} Claude Code runs in safe mode with no tools@comma{} which skips hooks@comma{} MCP servers and memory files@comma{} and Codex runs read-only and ignores the user's @samp{config.toml}@comma{} whose hooks and notification programs would otherwise fire; Codex still reads its login from @samp{CODEX_HOME}.

The user options @code{agent-log-summary-claude-program} and @code{agent-log-summary-codex-program} name the executables@comma{} @samp{"claude"} and @samp{"codex"} by default@comma{} looked up in @code{exec-path}.  The user option @code{agent-log-summary-codex-model} names the Codex model; when @code{nil} (the default)@comma{} Codex uses its built-in default model@comma{} since the user's @samp{config.toml} is not read.  The user option @code{agent-log-summary-cli-timeout} is the number of seconds a command-line run may take before it is killed and counted as a failed summary; the default is @code{120}.

The user option @code{agent-log-summary-backend} specifies the @samp{gptel} backend name to use for summary requests@comma{} for example @samp{"Gemini"} or @samp{"Claude"}.  When @code{nil} (the default)@comma{} the backend is inferred from @code{agent-log-summary-model} if set@comma{} otherwise it falls back to @code{gptel-backend}.

The user option @code{agent-log-summary-model} specifies the @samp{gptel} model to use@comma{} for example @code{claude-haiku-4-5-20251001}.  A smaller@comma{} faster model is often sufficient for summarization.  When @code{nil} (the default)@comma{} it uses @code{gptel-model}.

The user option @code{agent-log-summary-max-content-length} sets the maximum number of characters of conversation text sent to the LLM for summarization.  The package extracts the plain text of user and assistant messages (ignoring tool calls@comma{} thinking blocks@comma{} Claude local-command output@comma{} and injected system preambles) and stops collecting text once this limit is reached.  The default value is @code{8000}.  Increase this for longer@comma{} more detailed summaries at the cost of higher token usage and more foreground parsing; decrease it if you want to minimize API costs and UI disruption.

@node AI search options
@section AI search options

@vindex agent-log-search-scope-backend
@vindex agent-log-search-scope-model
@vindex agent-log-search-backend
@vindex agent-log-search-model
@vindex agent-log-search-budget

The AI search feature (@ref{AI search, , AI search}) uses two LLM calls: a scope-narrowing stage and a selection stage.  Each stage has its own backend and model options@comma{} allowing you to use a cheap@comma{} fast model for scope narrowing and a more capable model for the selection narrative.

The user option @code{agent-log-search-scope-backend} specifies the @samp{gptel} backend for stage 1 (scope narrowing)@comma{} for example @samp{"Gemini"} or @samp{"Claude"}.  When @code{nil} (the default)@comma{} the backend is inferred from @code{agent-log-search-scope-model} if set@comma{} otherwise it falls back to @code{gptel-backend}.

The user option @code{agent-log-search-scope-model} specifies the model for stage 1.  A small@comma{} fast model (e.g. @code{claude-haiku-4-5-20251001}) is recommended since the task is simple classification.  When @code{nil} (the default)@comma{} it uses @code{gptel-model}.

The user option @code{agent-log-search-backend} specifies the @samp{gptel} backend for stage 2 (selection and narrative).  When @code{nil} (the default)@comma{} the backend is inferred from @code{agent-log-search-model} if set@comma{} otherwise it falls back to @code{gptel-backend}.

The user option @code{agent-log-search-model} specifies the model for stage 2.  This model receives all filtered session summaries and must reason over them@comma{} so a more capable model may produce better results.  When @code{nil} (the default)@comma{} it uses @code{gptel-model}.

The user option @code{agent-log-search-budget} sets a per-request cost threshold as a @samp{(TYPE . LIMIT)} cons cell@comma{} where @samp{TYPE} is either @code{tokens} or @code{dollars}.  Before stage 2 sends the filtered summaries to the LLM@comma{} the package estimates the prompt's token count (heuristically@comma{} one token per four characters).  If the estimate exceeds the limit@comma{} the user is asked to confirm with @code{y-or-n-p}.  The default value is @code{(tokens . 50000)}.

In @code{dollars} mode@comma{} cost estimation requires the @samp{gptel-plus} package.  If @samp{gptel-plus} is not available@comma{} the budget is not enforced and a warning is displayed.  Dollar estimates use the model's input pricing from @samp{gptel-plus} to convert the estimated token count to a dollar amount.

