@node Session browsing
@section Session browsing

The project and session pickers remain usable if the buffer from which you opened them is closed while you are choosing an entry.

@findex agent-log-browse-sessions
@findex agent-log-open-latest
@findex agent-log-open-file
@findex agent-log-open-session
@findex agent-log-open-current-session

The command @code{agent-log-browse-sessions} is the primary entry point for exploring your session history.  It asks each active backend for its canonical session catalog@comma{} resolves each session to its exact JSONL file@comma{} and presents the results in a @code{completing-read} prompt.  For Codex@comma{} the catalog comes from app-server @samp{thread/list}.  Agent Log follows native Resume's database-first path so browsing never waits for a scan of the rollout archive.  If the index is stale@comma{} the current indexed catalog is shown immediately while app-server runs its normal scan-and-repair path in the background; another browse picks up the repaired index.  Repairs for the same newest rollout are rate-limited@comma{} so an active or otherwise temporarily unindexed session cannot restart an archive scan on every invocation.  The index is stale when it is empty@comma{} and also when it omits the newest rollout on disk: Codex indexes a thread when its session closes@comma{} so a session whose process was killed rather than closed — as @code{agent-restart} does — would otherwise stay out of the browser for good.  Checking this costs a few directory reads@comma{} because rollouts sit in a date-partitioned tree under names that begin with the session timestamp.  Subagent rollouts are skipped when looking for the newest one: Codex never indexes them@comma{} so measuring the index against one would report it stale forever.  In both cases app-server remains the membership authority: Agent Log applies no rule of its own and never infers sessions from rollout files.  Each candidate is formatted as:

@example
[icon] 2026-02-22 14:06  my-project  42k  "Fix the authentication bug in..."
@end example

Each candidate is prefixed with a backend-specific icon so you can tell at a glance which agent a session belongs to.  In GUI frames the icon is an inline SVG image; in terminal frames it falls back to a short text label (e.g. @samp{CC} for Claude Code@comma{} @samp{CX} for Codex).  Each distinct backend icon is rendered once per candidate set@comma{} using frame metrics without selecting a window@comma{} so candidate construction does not run unrelated window-selection hooks once per session.  The size column shows the human-readable size of the source JSONL file.

When @code{agent-log-group-by-project} is non-@code{nil} (the default is @code{nil})@comma{} the command first prompts for a project@comma{} then for a session within that project (@ref{Session browser options, , Session browser options}).  Sessions are sorted most-recent-first using @code{agent-log-session-sort-key}.  If AI summaries have been generated for any sessions (@ref{Summary generation, , Summary generation})@comma{} the summary replaces the quoted first message in the candidate display.  If the displayed session set still contains sessions without usable summaries while automatic summarization is enabled@comma{} browsing starts a non-overlapping background sweep that prioritizes that visible set@comma{} so the raw first-message entries are backfilled without waiting for AI search.

Every archive-wide command reads that same catalog@comma{} so what the session browser@comma{} @code{agent-log-open-latest}@comma{} bulk synchronization@comma{} archive summarization@comma{} background summary sweeps@comma{} and AI search treat as a session is exactly what the coding agent itself treats as a resumable conversation.  This matters most for Codex@comma{} which writes a separate rollout file for every subagent@comma{} permission guardian@comma{} and non-interactive execution thread@comma{} all inheriting the parent's working directory.  Codex leaves those out of its own thread list@comma{} so they never reach Agent Log and no heuristic is needed to recognize them.

Within a single browse run@comma{} source file size and modification-time metadata is cached per session.  This keeps flat browsing and modification-time sorting responsive even when the archive contains many sessions.  Checks for live sessions reuse the catalog that is already in hand instead of asking the backend for its session list a second time.  Live-state annotations use one snapshot of the agent package's session registry for the entire picker@comma{} rather than rescanning live buffers for each historical row.

Each project is labelled by the shortest trailing portion of its working directory that uniquely identifies it.  A project whose final directory component is unique is shown by that component alone (for example@comma{} @samp{my-project}); when several projects share a final component@comma{} each label grows one parent directory at a time until it is distinct (for example@comma{} @samp{8.1.0-dev/elpaca/sources/codex} versus @samp{8.3.0-dev/elpaca/sources/codex}).  This keeps labels short while guaranteeing that two distinct project directories never collapse to the same entry.  Labels that are still wider than @code{agent-log-project-column-max-width} are abbreviated in the browser (@ref{Session browser options, , Session browser options}).

Selecting a session lazily renders it to a @samp{.md} file if needed@comma{} then opens the rendered buffer in @code{agent-log-mode} (@ref{@code{agent-log-mode}, , @code{agent-log-mode}}).

The command @code{agent-log-open-latest} opens the most recent session directly@comma{} without prompting.  This is convenient when you want to review the last conversation you had with a coding agent.

The command @code{agent-log-open-file} opens a specific JSONL file by path.  It prompts for a file path interactively.  This is useful for automation@comma{} for opening session files you found via the filesystem@comma{} or when you know the exact file you want.

The command @code{agent-log-open-session} opens a session by its ID@.  It prompts for the session ID interactively.

The command @code{agent-log-open-current-session} opens the log for the coding agent session running in the current buffer.  When the @ref{Agent integration, , agent integration} is active@comma{} the buffer's authoritative native session id names the exact JSONL file first@comma{} which uniquely identifies the session even when multiple sessions (including branches created with @code{/branch}) share the same project directory.  Otherwise the command dispatches on the current buffer's backend: if the buffer is a Claude Code terminal@comma{} it matches distinctive visible terminal text against the project's JSONL transcripts@comma{} then falls back to the most recently modified JSONL file in the project's session directory@comma{} or to searching @samp{history.jsonl}.  If the buffer is a Codex terminal@comma{} @samp{agent-log} records the session ID reported by Codex hook events and uses that buffer-local ID first.  Resolving the buffer's session reads the Codex thread catalog only when the buffer's own records cannot name it@comma{} so the common case does not start an app-server process.  Buffers launched with an explicit @samp{codex resume SESSION_ID} command open that session ID directly@comma{} even if the resumed session was originally spawned as a subagent.  Otherwise the backend matches a Codex session whose recorded working directory matches the buffer's directory and whose @samp{session_meta} timestamp is near the live terminal process start time.  If that launch-time match fails but the terminal still displays transcript text from a completed Codex session@comma{} the backend normalizes distinctive visible lines (for example@comma{} terminal continuation indentation@comma{} list markers@comma{} and Markdown backticks)@comma{} matches them back to the same project's JSONL files@comma{} and opens the transcript that actually appears in the buffer.  This lets you quickly jump from an active or recently completed coding agent session to its rendered log without attaching a buffer to an unrelated child or stale session.

@node Rendered directory
@section Rendered directory

@findex agent-log-open-directory

The command @code{agent-log-open-directory} opens the rendered Markdown directory in Dired.  Since rendered files are plain Markdown organized by project@comma{} you can use standard Emacs tools to browse@comma{} search@comma{} and manage them.  For example@comma{} you can run @samp{consult-ripgrep} from this Dired buffer to search across all rendered sessions.

If the rendered directory does not yet exist@comma{} the command creates it.

@node Sync and rendering
@section Sync and rendering

@findex agent-log-sync-sessions
@findex agent-log-refresh

By default@comma{} rendering is lazy: sessions are rendered to @samp{.md} files on first access via @code{agent-log-browse-sessions} or @code{agent-log-open-file}.  The package maintains an index file (@samp{_index.el}) in the rendered directory that maps session IDs to rendered file paths and JSONL file sizes.  Rendered ownership is checked using the backend@comma{} full session ID@comma{} and exact source path.  When you access a session@comma{} the package compares the current JSONL file size and canonical rendered path against the indexed values; if either differs@comma{} the session is re-rendered.

Rendered output never contains NUL characters: any NUL that appears in conversation content (for example@comma{} in captured terminal output from a build tool) is replaced with the visible @samp{␀} symbol.  A single NUL byte would otherwise make Emacs visit the rendered file as binary@comma{} breaking display of non-ASCII characters and crashing integrations that serialize the buffer content as JSON@.

The command @code{agent-log-sync-sessions} renders all ordinary@comma{} non-ignored sessions that are unrendered or stale.  It parses and renders sessions one at a time in background Emacs subprocesses@comma{} then commits the shared index once.  Automatic single-session sync uses the same subprocess path; its initial index lookup and index update also run in the worker.  Workers inherit the rendering and redaction options and write private temporary files; Emacs publishes each completed render and updates open buffers.  If another operation updates the index@comma{} single-session sync prepares a fresh index update without repeating the render@comma{} retrying up to three times.  If the source or summary changes during rendering@comma{} a newer sync supersedes it@comma{} or index publication exhausts those retries@comma{} the stale result is rejected and the session remains eligible for a later sync.  When a session's rendered path changes@comma{} the superseded file it owned is moved to the operating system Trash; no other rendered file is touched.  Open session buffers follow the new rendered path and source watcher before the old file is removed@comma{} so live updates and buffer cleanup continue using the canonical file.  The completion callback reports a structured success or failure result@comma{} including the stage that failed.

The command @code{agent-log-refresh} re-renders the session displayed in the current buffer from its JSONL source.  This is useful if the rendered file appears stale or if you have changed display settings such as @code{agent-log-show-thinking} or @code{agent-log-show-tools} (@ref{Display of thinking and tool sections, , Display of thinking and tool sections}).  The keybinding is @samp{g} in @code{agent-log-mode}.

@node Navigation
@section Navigation

@findex agent-log-next-turn
@findex agent-log-previous-turn

The command @code{agent-log-next-turn} moves point to the next User or Assistant heading in the buffer.  The command @code{agent-log-previous-turn} moves to the previous one.  These commands search for @samp{## User} and @samp{## Assistant} headings@comma{} skipping over tool calls and thinking sections.

The keybindings are @samp{n} and @samp{p} in @code{agent-log-mode}@comma{} making it easy to step through a conversation turn by turn.

@node Section visibility
@section Section visibility

@findex agent-log-toggle-section
@findex agent-log-collapse-all
@findex agent-log-expand-all

The command @code{agent-log-toggle-section} toggles the visibility of the section at point.  When point is on a @samp{####} heading (tool call@comma{} tool result@comma{} or thinking block)@comma{} it collapses or expands just that section.  When point is on any other heading@comma{} it delegates to @code{outline-toggle-children}.  The keybinding is @samp{TAB}.

The command @code{agent-log-collapse-all} collapses all tool-use@comma{} tool-result@comma{} and thinking sections in the buffer at once.  This gives you a clean view of just the user messages and assistant text responses.  The keybinding is @samp{C}.

The command @code{agent-log-expand-all} expands all sections@comma{} removing all section overlays and showing the full content.  The keybinding is @samp{E}.

@node Copying
@section Copying

@findex agent-log-copy-turn

The command @code{agent-log-copy-turn} copies the current turn to the kill ring.  A ``turn'' is delimited by @samp{##} headings: from the current @samp{## User} or @samp{## Assistant} heading up to (but not including) the next one.  This is useful for extracting a specific message from a conversation to paste elsewhere.  The keybinding is @samp{w}.

@node Summary generation
@section Summary generation

@findex agent-log-summarize-sessions
@findex agent-log-stop-summarize-sessions

The command @code{agent-log-summarize-sessions} generates AI summaries for all ordinary sessions that do not yet have a current one.  With the default @code{agent-log-summary-method} it requires the @samp{gptel} package.  The command processes sessions sequentially@comma{} sending each conversation's text to the configured LLM or command-line tool (@ref{Summary generation (1), , Summary generation options}) and storing the response in the index.  Summary requests through @samp{gptel} disable @code{gptel-use-tools} so that globally configured tools (e.g. MCP servers) are not included in the request.

Each summary consists of two parts: a one-line summary (up to 80 characters) used in the session browser display@comma{} and a paragraph summary (3--5 sentences) shown at the top of rendered buffers.  The LLM is instructed to return a JSON object with @code{oneline} and @code{summary} fields.

Progress is displayed in the echo area@comma{} showing which pending summary update is running@comma{} how many sessions were discovered in the archive@comma{} which session is being summarized@comma{} and the model or command-line tool in use.  The pending-update count is the number of sessions lacking a current summary@comma{} not the total number of sessions in the archive.  If you invoke @code{agent-log-summarize-sessions} while summarization is already running@comma{} it stops the current run instead.  You can also stop explicitly with @code{agent-log-stop-summarize-sessions}.

Summaries are persisted in the index file together with a fingerprint and source file state for the conversation text they describe@comma{} so they survive across Emacs sessions and are refreshed only when the underlying conversation changes.  Rendered Markdown files also include the paragraph summary below the session header when a usable summary is available; if a background summary finishes after rendering@comma{} the rendered file is updated in place.  Because the summary is stored in the file itself@comma{} opening a log displays it exactly once rather than re-inserting it.  If the JSONL file state changes but the fingerprint still matches@comma{} the index metadata is refreshed in place without another LLM request on the per-session summarization path.  This matters for metadata-only rewrites@comma{} such as moving a Codex session log to a new project: the JSONL size and modification time can change even though the user/assistant conversation text has not.  Archive-wide commands do not preflight every stale fingerprint in the live Emacs process@comma{} because doing so can require rereading large transcripts before any LLM request starts.  Instead@comma{} archive-wide summarization and search treat stored summaries with a current-version conversation fingerprint as usable even when the cached JSONL size or modification time is stale; exact refresh remains on the targeted per-session path.  If you resume a previously summarized session and continue working@comma{} the next targeted summarization path treats the old summary as stale and generates a replacement.  The fingerprint covers all summary-relevant user and assistant text@comma{} not only the capped text sent to the LLM@comma{} so resumed work appended to a long session is still detected.  Legacy summaries with an older fingerprint are validated against the current conversation text and upgraded in place when that individual session is processed; summaries without a fingerprint are refreshed once because the package cannot prove what conversation text they describe.

@menu
* Automatic sync and summarization on session end::
@end menu

@node Automatic sync and summarization on session end
@subsection Automatic sync and summarization on session end

@vindex agent-log-auto-sync-sessions
@vindex agent-log-auto-summarize-sessions
@vindex agent-log-auto-summarize-sweep-interval
@vindex agent-log-auto-summarize-sweep-limit

These user options control what happens automatically when a Claude Code or Codex session stops producing output.  Claude Code requires a @samp{Stop} hook configured in Claude Code's settings (see the @samp{claude-code} documentation for hook setup).  Codex requires @samp{codex-mode} with @samp{codex-enable-hooks} enabled so @samp{codex-event-hook} receives @samp{Stop} events.

When @code{agent-log-auto-sync-sessions} is non-nil@comma{} the stopped session is rendered automatically.  If the event cannot be resolved to a specific session@comma{} automatic sync is skipped.  Automatic sync runs after every session end@comma{} so it keeps the echo area quiet: only a render failure the user may need to act on is shown there.  A render rejected because the session was still writing its log@comma{} which is routine while a session runs background work@comma{} is logged to the @samp{*Messages*} buffer only@comma{} and a later sync renders the session.

When @code{agent-log-auto-summarize-sessions} is non-nil@comma{} the stopped session is summarized automatically in a background @samp{emacs --quick --batch} subprocess@comma{} so the running Emacs stays responsive while the LLM request and index update complete.  The session-end path resolves the stopped session by file lookup and reads only that session's JSONL file for project@comma{} timestamp@comma{} and first-message metadata; it does not scan the full archive.  For a file larger than 1 MB it parses only the first megabyte@comma{} where that metadata lies@comma{} and parses the whole file only when that head has no user message; parsing a transcript of hundreds of megabytes in the running Emacs would freeze it in garbage collection.  The worker does not load the user's init file; it loads a private temporary state file containing the relevant @samp{agent-log} and@comma{} when summaries go through @samp{gptel}@comma{} @samp{gptel} runtime configuration@comma{} including whether @code{agent-log-auto-rename-sessions} is enabled@comma{} then deletes that file.  API keys never go into that file.  With the @samp{gptel} method@comma{} the running Emacs looks up the summary backend's key@comma{} calling it first if it is a function (which may rely on code the worker does not load)@comma{} and sends it to the worker on standard input.  With a command-line method@comma{} no key exists or is sent; the running Emacs first checks@comma{} asynchronously@comma{} which tool is installed and logged in@comma{} and the worker uses that tool.  Starting a worker is logged to the @samp{*Messages*} buffer only and leaves the echo area untouched@comma{} and successful workers finish silently; a worker in which any session's summary failed exits with a non-zero status@comma{} which is reported in the echo area.  If auto-sync is also enabled@comma{} the background summarizer is launched after sync completes.  If the event cannot be resolved to a specific session@comma{} automatic summarization is skipped quietly.  Workers start from @samp{temporary-file-directory} rather than the current buffer's directory@comma{} because they address their state file absolutely and never use the working directory; this keeps summarization working after a project directory has been deleted or renamed@comma{} which would otherwise stop the worker from starting at all.

@findex agent-log-repair-rendered-index

If older index entries point at files under the @samp{unknown} rendered project@comma{} run @code{M-x agent-log-repair-rendered-index}.  The command re-renders those sessions from their JSONL files@comma{} writes corrected project-path Markdown files@comma{} and updates the rendered index.  It does not delete the old @samp{unknown} files.

Automatic summarization also runs a periodic backlog sweep so sessions created outside the Emacs lifecycle hooks@comma{} such as external Codex invocations@comma{} are eventually summarized.  The sweep is controlled by @code{agent-log-auto-summarize-sweep-interval} (default 900 seconds) and @code{agent-log-auto-summarize-sweep-limit} (default 20 sessions per sweep).  The timer is self-rescheduling rather than a long-lived repeating timer@comma{} so stale triggered timer objects are replaced automatically after each firing.  Each sweep runs in a separate batch Emacs process; the running Emacs only starts the worker and does not scan or summarize the archive itself.  If AI search or session browsing sees missing usable summaries while automatic summarization is enabled@comma{} it repairs a stale sweep timer if needed and starts a non-overlapping sweep that prioritizes the session set that exposed the missing summaries before continuing.  Set @code{agent-log-auto-summarize-sweep-interval} to nil to disable backlog sweeps while keeping session-end summaries enabled.

The hook handlers (@code{agent-log-claude--session-end-handler} and @code{agent-log-codex--session-end-handler}) are defined in the backend files.  Setting either defcustom installs them as soon as @samp{agent-log} has loaded@comma{} so the automatic actions work from startup: the Claude handler is autoloaded@comma{} so installing it loads nothing and its backend loads on the first event@comma{} and the Codex backend is loaded to install its hooks.  The Codex backend also installs a session identity recorder on @code{codex-event-hook} and a start hook that clears stale buffer-local identity.  It rechecks these hooks before @samp{codex} dispatches lifecycle events@comma{} so another package resetting @code{codex-event-hook} does not silently disable automatic summaries.

Archive-wide manual summarization excludes sessions with a live agent process by session ID@.  Each backend's @code{agent-log--active-session-ids} method provides the IDs of currently running sessions.  For Codex buffers@comma{} the backend uses the same hook-recorded ID@comma{} resume-ID@comma{} project-plus-launch-time@comma{} and visible-transcript matching logic as @code{agent-log-open-current-session} (@ref{Session browsing, , Session browsing}).  Claude buffers whose @samp{claude-code-extras} status data has not been initialized yet are ignored until a session ID is available.  Targeted automatic summarization may summarize the stopped session even while its terminal buffer remains live@comma{} because the lifecycle event identifies that specific session.  If summarization is already running when another session stops@comma{} the in-progress batch continues undisturbed.

@node Session rename
@section Session rename

@findex agent-log-rename-sessions
@vindex agent-log-auto-rename-sessions

The user option @code{agent-log-auto-rename-sessions} controls whether sessions are renamed automatically when they receive an AI summary.  When set to non-@code{nil}@comma{} each time @code{agent-log-summarize-sessions} successfully generates a summary for a session@comma{} the one-line summary is slugified and written as a @samp{custom-title} entry in the session JSONL file.  This makes the name immediately visible in Claude Code's @samp{/resume} picker without any manual step.

The default value is @code{nil}.  Set it to @code{t} if you want session names to stay in sync with summaries automatically.  When a refreshed summary replaces an older summary-generated title@comma{} the title is refreshed too; titles that do not match the previous summary are treated as user-provided and left alone.

The command @code{agent-log-rename-sessions} renames all sessions that lack a custom title.  This command is defined in @samp{agent-log-claude.el} because custom-title entries are a Claude Code-specific JSONL convention.  For each session that has a one-line summary in the index but no @samp{custom-title} entry in its JSONL file@comma{} the command writes the summary directly as the custom title.  With a prefix argument (@samp{C-u})@comma{} it overwrites existing custom titles as well@comma{} which is useful after changing the title format.

This is a purely local@comma{} offline operation — no API calls are made.  Sessions must be summarized first via @code{agent-log-summarize-sessions} (@ref{Summary generation, , Summary generation}).  Sessions that already have a custom title (e.g. from Claude Code's @samp{/rename} command) are skipped.

After writing custom-title entries@comma{} the cached JSONL file sizes in the index are updated in a single bulk write so that @code{agent-log-sync-sessions} does not treat the modified files as stale and trigger unnecessary re-renders.

The command reports how many sessions were renamed@comma{} how many were skipped@comma{} and how many lacked a summary.

@node AI search
@section AI search

@findex agent-log-search
@findex agent-log-search-follow-link

The command @code{agent-log-search} searches your session history using natural language.  It prompts for a query string (e.g.@comma{} ``how did I implement the caching layer?'' or ``conversations about authentication in my web app last month'') and uses a two-stage LLM pipeline to find relevant sessions.

@strong{Stage 1: scope narrowing.}  The query@comma{} along with a summary of the archive's metadata (project names with session counts@comma{} date range)@comma{} is sent to a scope-narrowing model.  This model returns structured JSON specifying which projects and date range to include.  If the query does not mention a specific project or timeframe@comma{} all ordinary sessions are included.  The model and backend for this stage are configured via @code{agent-log-search-scope-model} and @code{agent-log-search-scope-backend} (@ref{AI search options, , AI search options}).

@strong{Stage 2: selection and narrative.}  The filtered current session summaries (one-line and paragraph@comma{} from @code{agent-log-summarize-sessions}) are sent to a selection model along with the original query.  If the number of matching sessions exceeds what fits within the token budget (@code{agent-log-search-budget})@comma{} only the most recent sessions are included.  The prompt includes a human-readable description of the scope applied in stage 1 (projects@comma{} date range@comma{} how many sessions matched@comma{} and how many were actually sent).  The model produces a Markdown narrative explaining which sessions are relevant and why@comma{} with inline links to specific sessions.  If the scope is narrower than ``all projects@comma{} all dates''@comma{} or if sessions were truncated for budget@comma{} the model notes this so you know some sessions may have been excluded.  Session summaries are sanitized to valid UTF-8 before serialization so that non-ASCII content does not cause errors.  The model and backend for this stage are configured via @code{agent-log-search-model} and @code{agent-log-search-backend} (@ref{AI search options, , AI search options}).

The result is displayed in a @samp{*agent-log-search*} buffer in @code{agent-log-search-mode} (@ref{@code{agent-log-search-mode}, , @code{agent-log-search-mode}}).  Session references in the narrative are rendered as clickable links.  Pressing @samp{RET} or clicking on a link opens the corresponding rendered session log.

Before starting@comma{} the command uses stored current-version fingerprinted summaries without rereading every transcript@comma{} even when cached JSONL file state is stale from a metadata-only rewrite.  If any sessions still lack usable summaries@comma{} it starts a background summary sweep when automatic summarization is enabled@comma{} warns that those sessions will be excluded from the current search@comma{} and asks for confirmation.  If no sessions have searchable usable summaries at all@comma{} the command aborts with a message to run @code{agent-log-summarize-sessions} first.  When existing summaries predate freshness tracking but cannot be preserved@comma{} the error says that those legacy summaries need refreshing rather than reporting them as never summarized.

Before stage 2@comma{} the estimated token cost is compared against @code{agent-log-search-budget} (@ref{AI search options, , AI search options}).  If the estimate exceeds the threshold@comma{} the user is asked to confirm.

When @code{gptel-plus} is available@comma{} the combined dollar cost of both stages is displayed at the bottom of the results buffer.

@node Session resumption
@section Session resumption

@findex agent-log-resume-session

The command @code{agent-log-resume-session} resumes the session displayed in the current buffer in the coding agent.  The command extracts the session ID from the buffer (either from the buffer-local variable or from the front-matter comment) and dispatches to the buffer's backend via the @code{agent-log--resume-session} generic function.  If a rendered Markdown file was opened directly rather than through @code{agent-log}@comma{} the command also reads the front-matter source path to recover the backend and source JSONL file before resuming.  For Claude Code sessions@comma{} this requires the @samp{claude-code} package and passes the session ID with the @samp{--resume} flag.  The session starts in the directory that its own transcript's folder under @samp{~/.claude/projects/} encodes@comma{} because that is where @samp{claude --resume} finds the transcript; the buffer you resume from plays no part unless no such directory exists.  Since the encoding is lossy@comma{} a matching directory recorded in the transcript is preferred over a search of the filesystem.  Only when the folder leads nowhere does the backend fall back to the current buffer's project@comma{} then to @samp{history.jsonl}.  The project directory is normalized with a trailing slash to match the format returned by @code{claude-code--directory}@comma{} ensuring that buffer names@comma{} concurrent session detection@comma{} and modeline display all work correctly.  For Codex sessions@comma{} this requires the @samp{codex} package.  Agent Log first verifies the ID against the current canonical catalog@comma{} then reads the exact transcript header to verify its identity and obtain its current project directory.  This makes an old rendered log resume in the new project after relocation@comma{} even while the catalog still names the old project.  An unreadable or mismatched header or unavailable project directory stops the resume.  Agent Log then passes both that ID and the catalog's exact transcript path to app-server @samp{thread/resume}.  It does not rescan rollouts or fall back to another same-ID file if the canonical path disappears.  The CLI backend uses @samp{codex resume} for the same catalog-validated ID@.  The keybinding is @samp{r} in @code{agent-log-mode}.  When the @ref{Agent integration, , agent integration} is active@comma{} resuming additionally behaves as described there: live sessions are switched to rather than duplicated@comma{} and new resumes are routed through the @samp{agent} dispatcher.

@node Agent integration
@chapter Agent integration

Agent Log owns the durable archive; the optional @uref{https://github.com/benthamite/agent, agent} package owns live session control (buffers@comma{} accounts@comma{} lifecycle state@comma{} teardown).  The bridge module @samp{agent-log-agent.el} connects the two when both are loaded@comma{} regardless of load order: @samp{agent-log.el} requires it only after both the @samp{agent} and @samp{agent-log} features are available@comma{} so loading Agent Log alone never loads or requires @samp{agent}@comma{} and Agent Log remains fully functional standalone.  The bridge uses only public @samp{agent} API and adds @samp{:around} methods to existing Agent Log generics@comma{} so removing it restores standalone behavior exactly.

With the bridge active:

@itemize
@item
Opening the current session's log resolves the transcript from the buffer's authoritative native session id@comma{} which @samp{agent} maintains from the Claude status line and Codex hook events.  The standalone heuristics (visible-text transcript matching@comma{} newest project JSONL@comma{} @samp{history.jsonl} project match@comma{} Codex process and launch-time matching) remain as fallbacks for buffers whose id is not yet known.
@item
The session browser tags sessions that are currently live with their state — @samp{[busy]}@comma{} @samp{[waiting]}@comma{} @samp{[background-waiting]}@comma{} or @samp{[unknown]} — in the @code{agent-log-live-state} face.  There are two extension points: @code{agent-log-live-session-info-function} answers about one session at a time@comma{} and @code{agent-log-live-session-info-table-function} returns a snapshot of every live session at once.  The bridge installs both@comma{} and the browser prefers the snapshot whenever it is installed@comma{} even if the snapshot comes back empty.  The tag is a column like any other: it is padded to the width of the widest tag on display@comma{} so the date@comma{} project@comma{} and size columns line up whether or not a row is live@comma{} and it disappears entirely when no displayed session is live.  Selecting a session still opens the rendered log; liveness only annotates.
@item
The @samp{agent} session switcher shows each live session's stored one-line summary after the session name@comma{} dimmed and aligned into a column.  The bridge supplies that text through @code{agent-session-annotation-functions}@comma{} the abnormal hook @samp{agent} runs when it builds the switcher; @samp{agent} itself knows nothing about summaries.  Loading the bridge adds its own function to that hook with @code{add-hook}@comma{} so an annotation function you installed yourself keeps working whichever loads first@comma{} and the switcher shows whichever of you answers first.  @code{remove-hook} takes the bridge's function back out if you would rather annotate the switcher some other way.  The text comes from @code{agent-log-session-oneline}@comma{} which reads an in-memory cache rather than the rendered index@comma{} so opening the switcher never waits on a multi-megabyte file.  A session shows no annotation at all when the archive has not summarized it yet@comma{} when it is marked as having no conversation to summarize@comma{} or when @samp{agent} does not yet know its native session id; such sessions look exactly as they did before.  Because the background summary sweep skips sessions it believes are live (see below)@comma{} a live session's annotation describes its conversation as of the last time it was summarized@comma{} which can be hours behind.
@item
Resuming a session that is already live switches to its existing buffer@comma{} with a message naming its state@comma{} instead of starting a duplicate process on the same conversation.  The buffer goes to the window already showing it@comma{} or else replaces the selected window@comma{} as agent's session switcher does; it never splits the frame.
@item
Resuming an inactive session is routed through @code{agent-start-session}@comma{} so the resumed process gets @samp{agent}'s account handling@comma{} lifecycle registration@comma{} teardown@comma{} and state tracking.  Codex resumes keep the catalog validation and exact-transcript-path behavior described in @ref{Session resumption, , Session resumption}.
@item
Live-session id listing (used to protect running sessions from background summarization) comes from @samp{agent}'s session registry instead of backend heuristics@comma{} which for Codex also avoids reading the thread catalog.
@end itemize

@findex agent-log-session-oneline
@findex agent-log-refresh-session-onelines
@vindex agent-log-oneline-refresh-idle-delay

The cache behind the switcher annotations is filled by @code{agent-log-refresh-session-onelines}@comma{} which rereads the rendered index and keeps only the one-line summaries@comma{} so a consumer that wants a label does not hold the whole index in memory.  The rebuild is skipped when the index file's size and modification time are unchanged since the last one.  The bridge runs the rebuild from a repeating idle timer whose delay is the user option @code{agent-log-oneline-refresh-idle-delay}@comma{} in seconds@comma{} defaulting to @samp{5}.  A repeating idle timer runs at most once per idle period@comma{} so the rebuild happens on each pause that lasts at least that long@comma{} not once every five seconds of a long pause.  A firing whose index is unchanged costs a single file-attribute check; a firing after a change rereads and reparses the whole index@comma{} which takes roughly 90 ms on a 6 MB archive.  Lower the delay to have a newly written summary reach the switcher after a shorter pause; raise it so that only longer pauses pay for the reparse.  Setting the option to @code{nil}@comma{} or to a number that is not positive@comma{} cancels the timer altogether@comma{} and the switcher then shows whatever the cache last held.  The option reschedules the timer when it is set@comma{} so a change made through Customize or @code{setopt} takes effect at once.

A corrupt rendered index makes the rebuild signal rather than replace good summaries with an empty cache@comma{} so the switcher keeps showing the annotations it already had.  Emacs reports the error from the timer as an ordinary message@comma{} once per pause rather than every few seconds; with @code{debug-on-error} set@comma{} each of those firings raises the debugger and shows a backtrace instead.  The timer survives the error@comma{} and the index's file state is recorded only after a successful rebuild@comma{} so every later firing rereads the whole index until the index is valid again — at which point annotations resume by themselves@comma{} with nothing to restart.

Browsing history stays lazy in both directions: the @samp{agent} transients never read the historical catalog@comma{} and building the switcher reads only memory.  The idle refresh above is the one piece of bridge work that runs on its own; everything else waits for an explicit action.  On the @samp{agent} side@comma{} the shared @code{agent-history} command (bound to @samp{l} in @code{agent-menu}) opens @code{agent-log-menu} on demand.

@node Secret redaction
@chapter Secret redaction

Because AI coding agents frequently see or handle secrets@comma{} their conversation logs can contain API keys@comma{} tokens@comma{} and other credentials in plain text.  The optional @samp{agent-log-redact} module scans each rendered entry for known secret formats and replaces every match with a deterministic placeholder of the form @samp{[REDACTED:LABEL:HASH8]}@comma{} where @samp{HASH8} is the first 8 hex chars of @samp{sha256(secret + salt)}.  The same secret always produces the same placeholder@comma{} so you can still reason about which credential appears where without exposing its value.

Enable it by loading the module:

@lisp
(require 'agent-log-redact)
@end lisp

Turning on @code{agent-log-auto-redact-sessions} (see @ref{User options (1), , User options}) loads it too@comma{} once @samp{agent-log} has loaded.

After enabling for the first time@comma{} run @code{agent-log-redact-rebuild-all} to re-render any previously-rendered logs through the redactor.

@menu
* Coverage::
* Integration::
* Commands: Commands (1). 
* User options: User options (1). 
@end menu

@node Coverage
@section Coverage

The built-in pattern list (@code{agent-log-redact-patterns}) covers AWS access keys@comma{} GitHub tokens (classic@comma{} @samp{ghs_}@comma{} and fine-grained PATs)@comma{} GitLab personal access tokens@comma{} Slack tokens@comma{} generic @samp{sk-} API keys (with a more specific @samp{sk-ant-} rule for Anthropic keys that fires first)@comma{} Google API keys (@samp{AIza...})@comma{} JSON Web Tokens@comma{} database connection strings with embedded credentials (@samp{postgres}@comma{} @samp{mysql}@comma{} @samp{mongodb}@comma{} etc.)@comma{} Google OAuth client secrets@comma{} PEM private-key blocks@comma{} and generic @samp{api_key} / @samp{password} / @samp{token} assignments with high-entropy values.  Add site-specific patterns via @code{agent-log-redact-extra-patterns} (same @samp{(REGEX . LABEL)} alist format); suppress known-safe matches via @code{agent-log-redact-allowlist} (a list of literal strings).

Patterns run against whole JSONL lines@comma{} which can be megabytes long.  To match across newlines@comma{} use a character class such as @samp{[[:ascii:][:nonascii:]]*?} rather than an alternation such as @samp{\(?:.\|\n\)*?}: the alternation pushes one backtrack point per character@comma{} and on a long line Emacs aborts the whole scrub with ``Stack overflow in regexp matcher''.

@node Integration
@section Integration

Redaction is applied at two layers:

@enumerate
@item
@emph{Render-time} via @samp{:filter-return} advice on @code{agent-log--render-entry}@comma{} so every rendered entry passes through the redactor on its way to the Markdown file.  Covers both the full-render path (@code{agent-log--render-to-file}) and the live-update path (@code{agent-log--append-rendered-line}).
@item
@emph{Source-level} via @code{agent-log-redact-scrub-jsonl}@comma{} which walks each .jsonl under @code{agent-log-redact-jsonl-roots} (Claude Code's @samp{~/.claude/projects/}@comma{} Codex's @samp{~/.codex/sessions/})@comma{} parses each line as JSON@comma{} and rewrites non-opaque string values through @code{agent-log-redact-text} atomically.  JSON object keys and values under @code{agent-log-redact-opaque-json-keys} are preserved unchanged.  Files modified within @code{agent-log-redact-active-session-seconds} are skipped to avoid racing with an active session.
@end enumerate

Source-level scrubbing is the authoritative redaction for plaintext transcript fields: once the JSONL is clean@comma{} every downstream rendering of that session is automatically clean@comma{} and there is no plain-text copy of the secret on local disk.  Session-resume remains safe only if authenticated or encrypted resume fields are preserved byte-for-byte; by default@comma{} keys such as @samp{encrypted_content}@comma{} @samp{signature}@comma{} @samp{ciphertext}@comma{} @samp{nonce}@comma{} @samp{mac}@comma{} @samp{tag}@comma{} and suffixed variants such as @samp{thinking_signature} are treated as opaque.

Neither layer is a substitute for preventing secrets from entering the transcript in the first place (that is the job of an upstream PreToolUse hook).  They are defense in depth: prevention at the edge@comma{} redaction at the core@comma{} and redaction at the view.

@node Commands (1)
@section Commands

@findex agent-log-redact-rebuild-all
@code{agent-log-redact-rebuild-all} deletes every rendered @samp{.md} file under @code{agent-log-rendered-directory}@comma{} clears the index@comma{} and re-runs @code{agent-log-sync-sessions} so every non-ignored canonical session is re-rendered through the redactor.  Use after enabling redaction or after changing the pattern list.

@findex agent-log-redact-existing-in-place
@code{agent-log-redact-existing-in-place} is a faster alternative for the first-time migration.  It iterates every already-rendered @samp{.md} file@comma{} runs its contents through @code{agent-log-redact-text}@comma{} and rewrites only files whose content actually changed — no JSONL re-parsing.  Much faster than @code{agent-log-redact-rebuild-all} on large archives.  Sessions whose JSONL has grown since their last render are not refreshed by this command; run @code{agent-log-sync-sessions} afterward to pick up any new content through the advice-based redactor.

@findex agent-log-redact-scrub-jsonl
@code{agent-log-redact-scrub-jsonl} is the source-level scrubber.  It walks every directory in @code{agent-log-redact-jsonl-roots} recursively@comma{} and for every @samp{.jsonl} older than @code{agent-log-redact-active-session-seconds} examines each line.  Idempotency is enforced at three layers.  First@comma{} @code{agent-log-redact-text} skips any regex match whose captured text already contains a @samp{[REDACTED:...]} substring@comma{} so a pattern with a permissive character class cannot swallow and re-hash its own placeholder (e.g. the @samp{db-password} pattern captures @samp{[^@@/ \n]+} as the password@comma{} which would otherwise match a previously-redacted placeholder on a subsequent pass).  Second@comma{} lines that contain no pattern match at all are left verbatim — no JSON round-trip.  Third@comma{} lines that match syntactically but whose recursive walk redacts no actual string are also left verbatim (this covers matches inside already-redacted placeholders and opaque fields such as Codex @samp{encrypted_content}).  Only lines where at least one non-opaque string value is actually rewritten trigger JSON re-serialization.  The file is rewritten atomically (temporary file + rename) only when at least one line actually changed.  Both fast paths are required because JSON parse + serialize is not guaranteed to be byte-identical for all valid inputs — unconditional re-serialization would cause spurious drift across re-runs.  Lines that fail to parse as JSON are preserved unchanged@comma{} so malformed or truncated entries survive scrubbing.  The write path disables Emacs lockfiles so concurrent scrubber invocations do not deadlock on stale @samp{.#} lock symlinks.  A file whose scrub signals an error is left unchanged and reported as @samp{failed to scrub FILE: ERROR}; the walk then continues with the next file@comma{} so one bad file cannot leave the rest unscrubbed.  The final summary line counts the failures@comma{} and the command returns the list of failed files.  A run scans only files modified since the last completed run@comma{} plus the files that run failed on; @code{agent-log-redact-scrub-state-file} records that run.  Every file is scanned on the first run@comma{} after the redaction patterns@comma{} allowlist or opaque-field settings change@comma{} and when the command is called with a prefix argument (or a non-nil @samp{FULL} argument).  Run once after enabling redaction to clean historical sessions@comma{} and periodically afterward (manually@comma{} or via a cron-like schedule) to scrub newly-completed sessions.

@findex agent-log-redact-preview
@code{agent-log-redact-preview} lists every potential match in the current buffer — with its label@comma{} position@comma{} and a truncated preview — in a dedicated report buffer.  Useful for tuning patterns or adding entries to @code{agent-log-redact-allowlist} when a legitimate value triggers a false positive.

@node User options (1)
@section User options

@vindex agent-log-redact-enabled
@code{agent-log-redact-enabled} (default @samp{t}) toggles redaction globally.  Set to @samp{nil} to bypass the redactor without unloading the module.

@vindex agent-log-redact-patterns
@code{agent-log-redact-patterns} is the built-in @samp{(REGEX . LABEL)} alist.  Each entry is applied in order.  If a regex contains a capture group@comma{} only the first capture group is replaced; otherwise the whole match is replaced.  Use a capture group when you want to preserve surrounding context (e.g. the @samp{password=} prefix in a @samp{password=VALUE} match).

@vindex agent-log-redact-extra-patterns
@code{agent-log-redact-extra-patterns} is appended to the built-in list on every scan.  Use it to add site-specific secret formats without editing the built-in.

@vindex agent-log-redact-allowlist
@code{agent-log-redact-allowlist} is a list of literal strings that are never redacted@comma{} even when matched by a pattern.  Use to suppress known false positives such as public test keys.

@vindex agent-log-redact-hash-salt
@code{agent-log-redact-hash-salt} is an optional salt prepended to secrets before hashing.  An empty salt (the default) is safe — the 8-char truncation already makes recovery impractical.  Set a non-empty salt if you sync the same logs to multiple machines and want placeholders to differ across them.

@vindex agent-log-redact-jsonl-roots
@code{agent-log-redact-jsonl-roots} lists the directories the source-level scrubber walks.  Defaults to Claude Code's @samp{~/.claude/projects/} and Codex's @samp{~/.codex/sessions/}.

@vindex agent-log-redact-opaque-json-keys
@code{agent-log-redact-opaque-json-keys} lists JSON keys whose values are never source-redacted.  This protects authenticated ciphertext and signature fields used by agent resume protocols.  Keys ending in @samp{_signature}@comma{} @samp{_ciphertext}@comma{} @samp{_nonce}@comma{} @samp{_mac}@comma{} or @samp{_tag} are also treated as opaque.

@vindex agent-log-redact-active-session-seconds
@code{agent-log-redact-scrub-state-file} is where the scrubber records its last completed run: the cutoff time@comma{} a hash of the redaction settings@comma{} and the files that failed.  Defaults to @samp{~/.claude/agent-log-redact-scrub-state.eld}.  Delete it to force a full rescan.

@code{agent-log-redact-active-session-seconds} controls how recently-modified a .jsonl must be before the scrubber skips it.  Default 300 (five minutes).  Prevents racing with a session that is still writing.

@vindex agent-log-auto-redact-sessions
@code{agent-log-auto-redact-sessions}@comma{} when non-nil@comma{} scrubs JSONL automatically after each Claude Code session ends.  The option belongs to @samp{agent-log} itself; @samp{agent-log-redact} loaded alone@comma{} as @samp{agent-remote} loads it@comma{} treats the unset option as off. Turning it on loads @samp{agent-log-redact} at startup@comma{} so both the scrub and the redaction of renders are active from the start rather than only once something else loads the module.  Installs a handler on @code{claude-code-event-hook} that fires on @samp{:type stop} and@comma{} after @code{agent-log-redact-active-session-seconds} plus 30 seconds@comma{} launches an @samp{emacs --batch} subprocess that runs @code{agent-log-redact-scrub-jsonl}.  Only one scrubber runs at a time: a session end during a run queues a single further run@comma{} which starts when the current one finishes.  The subprocess exits with status 1 when any file failed.  When it finishes@comma{} its summary line is logged to the @samp{*Messages*} buffer without appearing in the echo area@comma{} since a scrub follows every session end; on failure the summary line is shown in the echo area@comma{} together with the name of the hidden buffer = *agent-log-redact-scrub*=@comma{} which keeps the subprocess's full output until the next run.  The handler returns nil@comma{} so the stop event still reaches the hook's other functions@comma{} such as agent.el's@comma{} which uses it to learn that a turn ended.  Using a subprocess is essential: scrubbing thousands of files inline in the interactive Emacs would block the UI for minutes.  Codex has no equivalent event hook@comma{} so Codex sessions are only scrubbed on manual invocation.  This is the companion to @code{agent-log-auto-sync-sessions} and @code{agent-log-auto-summarize-sessions} — same pattern@comma{} different action.

@node @code{agent-log-mode}
@chapter @code{agent-log-mode}

@findex agent-log-mode

@code{agent-log-mode} is the major mode used for rendered session buffers.  It derives from @code{markdown-view-mode}@comma{} inheriting Markdown fontification in a read-only buffer.  Buffers are never marked as modified@comma{} so @samp{q} (@code{quit-window}) closes them without prompting to save.  The mode activates @code{outline-minor-mode} with a regexp that matches Markdown headings (=## =@comma{} =### =@comma{} =#### =)@comma{} enabling section folding.

Two invisibility specs are added: @code{agent-log-collapsed} (which shows the heading but hides the body) and @code{agent-log-hidden} (which hides both heading and body).  These are used by the section visibility commands (@ref{Section visibility, , Section visibility}) and the display configuration options (@ref{Display of thinking and tool sections, , Display of thinking and tool sections}).

When a buffer in this mode is killed@comma{} the file watcher is cleaned up and the index is updated with the final JSONL file size.

