@node Backend architecture
@section Backend architecture

The package uses a generic interface to support multiple AI coding agents.  The base type @code{agent-log-backend} (defined with @code{cl-defstruct}) provides shared slots for the backend name@comma{} key symbol@comma{} root directory@comma{} and rendered directory.  Each backend extends this type via @code{:include} and implements a set of generic functions (@code{cl-defgeneric} / @code{cl-defmethod}) that handle backend-specific operations: session discovery@comma{} JSONL entry normalization@comma{} conversation filtering@comma{} tool input summarization@comma{} and session resumption.

Backend files are loaded lazily.  When a backend is first accessed (e.g. during @code{agent-log-browse-sessions})@comma{} the package calls @code{require} on the backend's feature name and the backend registers a singleton instance in a global registry.  The registry maps backend key symbols to struct instances@comma{} so all dispatch goes through the struct's type.

The rendering pipeline@comma{} session browser@comma{} live update mechanism@comma{} AI summaries@comma{} and AI search all operate on a normalized intermediate representation.  Each backend's @code{agent-log--normalize-entries} method converts its native JSONL format into canonical plists that the generic code expects.  Core functions such as @code{agent-log--render-to-file} and @code{agent-log--extract-conversation-text} receive a backend instance and thread it through to generic dispatch points (conversation filtering@comma{} entry classification@comma{} tool summarization@comma{} text extraction).  The convenience function @code{agent-log--read-all-sessions} returns merged sessions from all active backends@comma{} sorted most-recent-first; individual buffers store their backend in the buffer-local variable @code{agent-log--backend}@comma{} set when a session is opened.  This means the core of the package is backend-agnostic: adding a new backend requires only implementing the generic functions@comma{} with no changes to the core file.

@node Data sources
@section Data sources

Each backend reads conversation data from a different directory layout.

@menu
* Claude Code::
* Codex::
@end menu

@node Claude Code
@subsection Claude Code

The Claude Code backend reads from two places:

@enumerate
@item
@samp{~/.claude/projects/<encoded-path>/<uuid>.jsonl}: one file per session@comma{} containing all messages as JSON lines.  These files define which sessions exist@comma{} because clients such as Claude Desktop never write the history file.
@item
@samp{~/.claude/history.jsonl}: the prompts typed in the terminal client.  It supplies a session's display text@comma{} and a project or timestamp only for a transcript that records none.  The file may be absent.
@end enumerate

A transcript is listed unless @samp{claude --resume} (as of version 2.1.318) would leave it out: its first 64 KB show a subagent sidechain@comma{} a team session@comma{} or a daemon session; its first 64 KB or last 64 KB show an Agent SDK entrypoint (@samp{sdk-cli}@comma{} @samp{sdk-ts}@comma{} @samp{sdk-py}); its first prompt is @samp{/loop}; or it holds no conversation message.  Two of the picker's filters are not applied.  One hides a session whose recorded directory differs from the current project but encodes to the same folder name; it belongs to a per-directory picker@comma{} and Agent Log lists all projects at once.  The other hides a session that continued in a background session.  The successor can hold only a compacted copy@comma{} so hiding the original could make a conversation impossible to find; both are listed@comma{} even when they largely repeat each other.

Listing stays cheap on a large store because a transcript is read only in bounded windows@comma{} never whole: its first and last 64 KB@comma{} and its first megabyte when the first 64 KB lack a working directory@comma{} a user prompt@comma{} or a message.  The result is cached against the file's size and modification time@comma{} so an unchanged transcript costs one file status lookup.  An interactive Emacs keeps these results in @samp{_claude-transcript-scans.el} in the rendered directory@comma{} so only the very first listing scans every transcript; on a store of 4@comma{}400 transcripts and 10 GB that scan took about six seconds@comma{} and later listings under a tenth of a second.  Only the part of @samp{history.jsonl} appended since the last read is parsed.

A session belongs to the project it first recorded@comma{} so a chat resumed elsewhere@comma{} or a newer chat with another working directory in the same transcript folder@comma{} does not relabel it.  If the recorded directory no longer exists@comma{} the session takes the folder's most recent working directory instead@comma{} which follows a project rename.  One listing checks each distinct directory once@comma{} because most sessions share a few directories and checks on a synced drive are slow.

The encoded path replaces every character other than an ASCII letter or digit with @samp{-}.  The package tries both the expanded path and its @code{file-truename} when locating a session directory@comma{} which handles symlinked project paths.

@node Codex
@subsection Codex

The Codex backend obtains session identity from Codex app-server @samp{thread/list} and reads conversation content from the exact rollout path returned for each thread.  Rollouts normally live under @samp{~/.codex/sessions/YYYY/MM/DD/rollout-<date>-<uuid>.jsonl}@comma{} or under the equivalent @samp{sessions/} tree in the active account's resolved @samp{CODEX_HOME}.

Each line in a Codex session file is an envelope @code{@{timestamp@comma{} type@comma{} payload@}}.  The @code{type} field determines how the entry is processed: @code{session_meta} entries provide session metadata (working directory@comma{} model@comma{} CLI version@comma{} and source); @code{response_item} entries carry the conversation content (user and assistant messages@comma{} function calls and their outputs@comma{} web searches@comma{} reasoning blocks); @code{event_msg} and @code{turn_context} entries are internal bookkeeping and are skipped during rendering.  The normalization step converts this envelope format to the canonical flat representation used by the rendering pipeline.  The interactive Emacs reads Agent Log's persistent Codex catalog cache and never contacts app-server while browsing.  When that cache is absent or omits the newest non-subagent rollout on disk@comma{} Agent Log starts a detached refresh and repair@comma{} rate-limited per rollout.  A separate batch Emacs performs the complete app-server handshake@comma{} paginates the native catalog@comma{} and atomically replaces the cache only after a successful response.  Its output is redirected at the operating-system level before it starts@comma{} and an operating-system timeout escalates to a forced stop if necessary.  App-server startup@comma{} rollout scanning@comma{} output processing@comma{} and timeout enforcement therefore occur outside the interactive Emacs.  If no successful cache exists yet and a refresh is running@comma{} the first browse reports that Codex sessions are initializing in the background; later browses use the completed cache.  If the refresh cannot start or fails@comma{} for example because neither @samp{timeout} nor @samp{gtimeout} is installed (on macOS@comma{} @samp{gtimeout} comes with coreutils)@comma{} the session browser shows the reason in its prompt until a later refresh succeeds or the catalog is current again.  Agent Log uses the cached native catalog's project@comma{} creation time@comma{} preview@comma{} and exact path rather than reconstructing identity from rollout metadata.  Resuming a session that is absent from the cache starts or joins a detached catalog refresh@comma{} then resumes automatically after native discovery confirms that exact session.  This applies both to the direct command and the @samp{agent} dispatcher.  Repeated requests for the same session share one pending resume@comma{} which survives closure of the launching log buffer.  An account change cancels the pending launch.  A failed refresh reports that availability is unknown; only a successful refresh can report that a session is absent.

@node Rendered directory structure
@section Rendered directory structure

The rendered directory mirrors the project structure:

@example
~/.claude/rendered/
  tango-wiki/
    2026-02-04_14-06_fix-the-authentication-bug--codex--019c....md
    2026-02-05_09-22_refactor-database-schema--claude-code--8d3e....md
  dotfiles/
    2026-02-22_11-56_redesign-agent-log-package--codex--019d....md
  _index.el
@end example

Each filename includes UTC creation time@comma{} a display slug@comma{} the backend@comma{} and the full session ID@.  Each @samp{.md} file contains HTML-comment front matter (session ID@comma{} backend@comma{} exact source path@comma{} rendered timestamp@comma{} JSONL size) followed by the full conversation rendered as Markdown.  The @samp{_index.el} file is a hash table mapping session IDs to metadata (rendered file path@comma{} JSONL size@comma{} and optionally a summary plus the conversation fingerprint and JSONL file state it describes).  The parsed table is cached in memory against the file's size and modification time@comma{} so a single browse that consults it several times parses the multi-megabyte file once@comma{} while a write by another process@comma{} such as a summary sweep worker@comma{} is picked up on the next read.  Summary commands use that metadata to distinguish current summaries@comma{} sessions explicitly marked as having no conversation@comma{} older fingerprinted summaries that can be validated and upgraded without another LLM request@comma{} no-fingerprint summaries that must be refreshed once@comma{} and stale summaries that need to be regenerated.

The maintenance command @code{agent-log-rendered-index-audit} reports those states separately@comma{} including source-present missing summaries@comma{} rendered-only historical entries whose original JSONL source is gone@comma{} stale index rows with no rendered file@comma{} remaining @samp{unknown} folder entries@comma{} and known test fixture rows.  @code{agent-log-clean-test-index-entries} removes known stale fixture IDs from the real rendered index when they have no existing rendered file.  @code{agent-log-recover-rendered-only-summaries} can generate summaries from rendered Markdown for historical entries whose JSONL source has disappeared; these summaries are marked with @code{:summary-source :rendered-markdown} and @code{:summary-recovered-from-rendered t} so they are not confused with canonical JSONL-backed summaries.

@node Rendering pipeline
@section Rendering pipeline

The rendering pipeline operates in three stages: parse@comma{} normalize@comma{} filter.  First@comma{} the JSONL file is parsed into raw plists.  Then each backend's @code{agent-log--normalize-entries} method converts the raw entries to a canonical format (for Claude Code this is a no-op since the native format is already canonical; for Codex it unwraps the envelope@comma{} converts content types@comma{} strips system XML@comma{} and merges consecutive entries of the same role into single turns).  Finally@comma{} the backend's @code{agent-log--filter-conversation} and @code{agent-log--conversation-entry-p} methods select @code{user} and @code{assistant} entries while excluding system-generated messages.  For Claude Code@comma{} system entries are those whose content starts with known XML tags such as @samp{<local-command-stdout>} or @samp{<command-name>}.  For Codex@comma{} system entries are those starting with @samp{<environment_context>}@comma{} @samp{<permissions>}@comma{} @samp{<turn_aborted>}@comma{} or @samp{<collaboration_mode>}.

Filtered entries are rendered as Markdown with @samp{##} headings for conversation turns and @samp{####} headings for tool calls and thinking blocks.  User turns show any text content followed by tool results.  Assistant turns show thinking blocks@comma{} text content@comma{} and tool-use summaries.

The session header (the @samp{# Session: ...} line at the top of each rendered file) derives its project name from the @code{progress} entry in the JSONL@comma{} which contains the working directory.  Not all sessions have a @code{progress} entry; when one is absent@comma{} the package falls back to the working directory@comma{} first timestamp and first user text found near the top of the transcript@comma{} and for whichever of those the transcript lacks@comma{} to @samp{history.jsonl}@comma{} looked up by session ID@.  The session date prefers the first conversation turn timestamp@comma{} falling back to the progress or session metadata timestamp when a log has been created but no user message has been recorded yet.

@node Live update mechanism
@section Live update mechanism

When @code{agent-log-live-update} is non-@code{nil} and a rendered buffer is open@comma{} the package registers a @code{file-notify} watcher on the source JSONL file.  When the file grows@comma{} the watcher reads only the new bytes (from the previously recorded offset to the new file size)@comma{} handling incomplete UTF-8 sequences at chunk boundaries.  New complete lines are parsed and rendered incrementally@comma{} appended to both the buffer and the @samp{.md} file on disk.  Partial lines (those not terminated by a newline) are buffered and completed when the next chunk arrives.

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

@menu
* No sessions found::
* Rendered files not updating::
* Live updates not working::
@end menu

@node No sessions found
@section No sessions found

Ensure that @code{agent-log-directory} points to the correct configuration directory (@ref{Directory configuration, , Directory configuration}).  The package looks for session files under @samp{projects/} in that directory.  A transcript that @samp{claude --resume} would not offer@comma{} such as an Agent SDK run@comma{} is not listed; open it with @code{agent-log-open-file}.

@node Rendered files not updating
@section Rendered files not updating

If a rendered file appears stale@comma{} press @samp{g} (@code{agent-log-refresh}) in the buffer to force a full re-render (@ref{Sync and rendering, , Sync and rendering}).  You can also delete the index file (@samp{_index.el} in the rendered directory) to force re-rendering of all ordinary sessions on next access.

@node Live updates not working
@section Live updates not working

Verify that @code{agent-log-live-update} is set to @code{t} (@ref{Live updates, , Live updates}).  Live updates rely on @code{file-notify}@comma{} which requires OS-level file watching support.  On some systems (e.g.@comma{} remote filesystems via TRAMP)@comma{} file notifications may not be available.

